plan FlowRuntime
title   Closed applications, readiness, actor ownership and causal context
status  proposed
goal
    Give hosts, React, requests, Stories and CLI one production Runtime: one admitted machine
    universe, one canonical store, one ordered decision path and truthful cleanup. This Plan owns
    FlowState O3 and O4 and decisions D8 to D10 (Runtime boundaries), D12 to D16, D27, D28 and
    D51. Their replacement decisions below name the former IDs. No implementation completion is
    claimed. Archived ApplicationsAndActors is provenance only; references/ledger/Runtime.md
    records every assigned inventory row.

    Machine turns and snapshots are @plan.Machines.O1 to @plan.Machines.O7; operation ownership
    is @plan.FlowOperations.O1 to @plan.FlowOperations.O7; storage and restore are @plan.FlowPersistence.O1
    to @plan.FlowPersistence.O6. React attachment is @plan.React.O1 to @plan.React.O3, hydration is
    @plan.ServerRendering.O1 to @plan.ServerRendering.O7, and lazy adoption is
    @plan.LazyFeatures.O1 to @plan.LazyFeatures.O3. Design rationale and examples are in
    references/design/minimal-library.md sections 1, 2, 9 and 10, and
    references/design/lazy-features.md sections 3, 5 to 9. Binding decisions.md rulings win over
    older design text, including ruling 38's deletion of shared multi-tab persistence.
outcomes
    O1  Apps
        app({ id, machines }) returns an inert App with the exact inferred keyed machine record;
        no public App.M alias is required. Each entry is a Machine, { machine, context? }, or
        { id, load, context? } for a declared lazy machine (@plan.LazyFeatures.O1). An eager entry
        uses exact machine-object provenance. A lazy entry declares its machine's literal id;
        load returns Promise<Machine> with that same literal id. First adoption fixes its exact
        object, and later mismatched objects reject with EntryMismatch. Construction never calls
        a loader. Ordinary ensure/create adopt synchronously from code already in hand; tooling
        and transferred SSR entries may load asynchronously under @plan.LazyFeatures.O2.

        The entry set is closed for the Runtime lifetime. No arbitrary runtime registration,
        dynamic roots or child actors exist. Adoption binds code to a declared id; it never adds
        an undeclared machine. Each eager or adopted machine contributes its context and send
        edges, descriptors, domains, entities and service requirements to the compiled graph.
        Unknown lazy entries stay inactive, with dormant storage bytes under
        @plan.LazyFeatures.O3. No per-entry Layer, loading actor lifecycle or public load command
        is added. Inspection's complete structural load does not activate a Runtime entry.

        A local actor inherits the machine entry's context default. A shared ref's context
        supplies its declared slots, falling back slot by slot to that default. The resolved
        record must cover every declared slot exactly; unknown slots or incompatible provider
        machines reject. ensure/create/hook call sites cannot supply wiring.

        Construction returns values and throws FlowError with canonical path-bearing diagnostics
        on ordinary malformed configuration, unknown fields, invalid entry kinds, duplicate keys
        where observable, duplicate durable machine ids (including lazy declared ids), or one
        machine value under two app keys. Across eager or adopted structure, reject colliding
        operation, resource, domain or entity identities, a second entity object for one id, one
        operation in two domains, or more than one byKey loader for an entity. Lazy conflicts are
        checked atomically at adoption before registration or work.

        App, machine, descriptor and stable-ref authored names use @plan.Machines.O1: nonempty,
        at most 256 UTF-8 bytes; no C0 controls, DEL, NUL or lone surrogates; no normalization or
        locale comparison. The app property key is ergonomic TypeScript structure; renaming it
        never renames a machine in persistence or traces. Durable identity uses app id, machine
        id and encoded actor id, never an app property key or a tooling group.

        Compilation and all app/setup/ref constructors create no actor, subscription, storage
        access, service acquisition, operation, timer or evidence. They may check static structure;
        they do not run value callbacks. Machine authoring's single builder invocation is
        @plan.Machines.O1, not execution during app compilation.
        Reference: references/dispatch-desk/app/app.ts.
    O2  Setup and readiness
        runtimeSetup({ app, services?, persistence? }).construct() is synchronous, service-free
        and inert. It returns a new isolated Runtime, not an acquired service environment.
        services is an Effect Layer. Its output must cover the inferred union of requirements
        from every reachable operation adapter, including every declared lazy entry's loader
        result type. Requirements propagate descriptor to machine to app, covariantly, without
        public recursive authored-config generics. Remove only Flow-owned Scope from consumer
        requirements. Passive reads, pure callbacks and context selectors add no service
        requirements. Complete supersets are valid; missing services or unresolved Layer input
        requirements are type errors. services may be omitted exactly when nothing is required;
        configured persistence also requires KeyValueStore. No RequirementsOf or Implementation
        compatibility surface is restored.

        One Runtime owns one Layer build scope and memoization context. Each service acquisition
        runs at most once in that build, including storage dependencies shared with adapters.
        Production composition uses Effect 4.0.5 Layer merge semantics, including its duplicate
        tag precedence; Flow adds no production duplicate-tag rejection. flow check warns when
        duplicates are statically visible. Fixtures' explicit overriding and duplicate checks are
        @plan.Testing.O3, not an alternative Runtime kernel.

        ready() is the sole readiness Effect. Executing it the first time starts one
        Runtime-owned bootstrap. Concurrent and later callers join the same cached terminal Exit,
        including Failure, Defect or Interruption; no implicit retry occurs. Interrupting one
        waiter interrupts only its wait. Runtime disposal interrupts bootstrap, closes admission
        and wins over a late service acquisition; nothing can reopen a disposed Runtime.

        Bootstrap order is binding:

        1. For persistence in a browsing context with navigator.locks, request the nonwaiting
        ifAvailable Web Lock for (app id, scope), encoded unambiguously. Hold a granted lock
        through the final write and disposal. If unavailable, report SecondTabNoPersistence and
        run this Runtime without storage restore or capture. With no navigator.locks, including
        a server or insecure origin, skip this check and proceed with persistence (ruling 38).
        2. Acquire KeyValueStore and its dependency closure before reading or validating persisted
        data. Read and prepare detached data, checking app/scope/version, codec bounds, identities,
        Schemas, durable context closure and safe-integer headroom. Dormant lazy records stay
        bounded and detached. Invalid scope data follows @plan.FlowPersistence.O4 and
        @plan.FlowPersistence.O5: discard with a diagnostic, then start fresh. No actor or canonical
        store entry is installed by detached preparation.
        3. Acquire the remaining application services in the same build scope, reusing storage
        dependencies; install validated store facts and restored actors, providers before
        consumers. Required SSR lazy entries load and adopt under @plan.LazyFeatures.O4 and
        transferred facts obey @plan.ServerRendering.O5.
        4. Establish complete silent context and reaction baselines, finish wiring validation,
        seal the admitted closure, activate actors, publish complete truth, then expose ordinary
        handles and open host admission. An unused restored actor's retain countdown starts here.

        Without persistence, omit storage/lock work. A storage acquisition failure is a readiness
        failure, not corrupt-data recovery. Any Layer acquisition failure fails ready() with
        FlowError and the original classification and Cause, rolls acquired owners back in
        reverse order, and never becomes an operation outcome. No partial handle, installed
        actor/store prefix or accepted turn evidence escapes failed readiness. Cleanup failure
        retains its truth and quarantine rather than pretending rollback succeeded.

        Before successful readiness, imperative ensureActor/createActor reject with NotReady;
        ordinary host commands cannot enter. Pure pre-attach handles used by React are the sole
        exception (@O4). Hydrate's inert render overlay and first-render rule are
        @plan.ServerRendering.O4; they do not require a mutable public boot API or render-time
        ready(). FlowProvider readiness/fallback/error behavior is @plan.React.O1.

        runtime.runPromise runs Effects with the installed services at a host boundary and can
        execute ready() itself without a circular readiness requirement. It is not another
        bootstrap API; application service use requires readiness. Use
        runPromise(Effect.exit(effect)) for an Exit; runPromiseExit is deleted. Phases, decoder,
        bootstrap machinery and mutable hydration remain private. All storage and Layer scopes
        close after their dependent actors and the final persistence closeout.
        Reference: references/dispatch-desk/app/setup.ts, app/host.ts, app/persistence.ts.
    O3  Actor identity
        actorRef(machine, id, { persist?, retain?, context? }) is inert and branded to the exact
        machine. id is a string or a readonly array of strings (ruling 35). Each authored string
        obeys @O1's name rules; a parts array is nonempty and each part is validated separately.
        String ids retain the wire form
        actor:<utf8ByteLength>:<machine-id><utf8ByteLength>:<authored-id>.
        Parts encode a distinct tagged sequence with each exact UTF-8 part length-prefixed and
        the part count, so ["a/b", "c"] differs from ["a", "b/c"] and a string cannot impersonate
        a parts array. No delimiter joining or normalization occurs. flow check warns on joined
        composite ids. Exact byte vectors, including the tagged parts encoding, are a foundation
        gate; an ambiguous encoding cannot ship.

        Shared identity within a Runtime is machine plus encoded id. persist (default false),
        retain and resolved context are metadata, not additional identities. Two ref values with
        the same identity but different persist/retain/context reject with a conflicting-ref
        FlowError, leaving the accepted actor, input and wiring unchanged. Compare durations by
        decoded duration and provider refs by exact admitted identity, not wrapper-object identity.
        Statically visible conflicts are also a flow check finding. A ref carries no input,
        callbacks, subscription or disposal authority. A local actor has only an opaque
        Runtime-local identity: it has no durable ref, cannot persist or become an actor-send
        target, and cannot be converted to a shared ref.

        runtime.ensureActor(ref, { input }) restores or creates the one shared actor for that
        identity. An input-free machine accepts runtime.ensureActor(ref) with no options. Normal
        admission is synchronous, and concurrent/reentrant requests for the same admission join
        one result. The first successfully accepted fresh input initializes once. Later ensures
        never reset memory; a different later input reports a diagnostic, rather than silently
        suggesting the actor now represents different data. Compare using private identity
        metadata; do not retain the original input in snapshots, callbacks, traces or persisted
        records (@plan.Machines.O1). An invisible failed admission does not pin input: after clean
        reverse rollback, a later call can retry with new input.

        Each admission validates readiness, exact App/machine/ref provenance, input Schema,
        encodability for a persisted ref, resolved complete wiring, available compatible provider
        incarnations, cycles, registry capacity and terminal counter headroom before registration
        or work. Unlisted/foreign machines and forged carriers fail closed; lazy entries first
        satisfy @plan.LazyFeatures.O2. Stage baseline and edges, attach/activate, then expose the
        handle last. Failure reverses staged edges, registry, mailbox and work in reverse order;
        no partial handle, snapshot or accepted occurrence escapes. A post-visibility failure uses
        ordinary disposal, including cleanup quarantine.

        runtime.createActor(machine, { input }) always creates a fresh anonymous local actor,
        wired from the app default and active before ordinary host exposure. It accepts no stable
        id or call-site wiring. Hosts own its disposal; React owns component-local disposal.
        Handles expose ref (opaque locally), stable send(event): void, getSnapshot() and snapshots;
        no lease, receipt, public acknowledgment or host getActor exists.

        Disposal followed by ensure creates a new incarnation, even for the same ref. Ensure
        while disposing waits for the old cleanup result before admitting the replacement; it
        never revives the old actor. A failed cleanup makes ensure re-report the cached quarantine
        error. The execution form of that exceptional wait is @Q1. Every operation outcome,
        schedule firing, issue clear/publication, private acknowledgment and actor send-back is
        fenced by incarnation. Old work cannot affect a replacement or inherit its issue authority.

        A use is an attached React actor consumer (ensuring or lookup hook), a live snapshots
        subscription, or a consumer's fixed context binding to the provider. getSnapshot and
        rendering a provisional handle are passive and acquire no use. Imperative ensure is a
        point use: it starts or resets idle retention but does not create a permanent caller
        lease; sends admitted to an active actor similarly refresh the idle deadline. Queued or
        running work is not a permanent use; disposal handles it truthfully under @O6.

        Without retain a shared actor lives until explicit or Runtime disposal. With retain,
        start its decoded Duration.Input deadline on the Runtime Clock when its last use ends,
        or at successful ensure/ready when it has no continuing use. A new use cancels the idle
        deadline; the next last-use release starts a full interval. Zero retain expires at the
        next scheduler opportunity after release, never in the middle of publication. Provider
        bindings prevent expiry. Retain is not suspension and cannot resurrect a disposing actor.
        Reference: references/dispatch-desk/features/Tickets/editor/editor.ref.ts, app/host.ts.
    O4  Mailboxes and send
        One FIFO mailbox per actor orders public events, internally authorized events, outcomes,
        schedules, stream/store facts, context wakeups and lifecycle facts. Reentrant sends append;
        nothing invokes another machine's handler inside the current turn. Machine macrosteps are
        @plan.Machines.O3. Production has no cross-turn execution budget; flow check warns on
        static send cycles, while Story process() stops at its private 10,000-turn budget
        (@plan.Testing.O2).

        The ordinary pending decision queue has capacity 1,024 facts per actor, excluding its
        current decision. External send is synchronous admission, returns void and throws
        FlowError(AdmissionOverloaded) before occurrence allocation, mutation, work or evidence
        when capacity is unavailable. Validate exact public tag and payload Schema on admission;
        forged/internal/invalid envelopes throw InvalidEvent and create no turn. Foreign Runtime
        handles and ref metadata conflicts reject before mutation. Sends to disposing/disposed
        handles are dropped with SendTargetAbsent, never buffered for a replacement incarnation.

        Internal producers obey the same finite capacity. No completion, timer, context or stream
        source gets an unbounded auxiliary queue. Runtime-owned producers wait for capacity or
        coalesce replaceable current facts without blocking the current turn's publication;
        accepted events retain their order. A non-backpressurable producer that cannot retain its
        fact within the bound reports AdmissionOverloaded on its owner and closes that source;
        it never silently claims delivery or fabricates success. Terminal cleanup has reserved
        capacity and may retire undecided facts (@O6), so saturation cannot prevent disposal.

        React ensure forms always return a stable handle before attach: default states,
        initial(input), Pending/placeholder lanes and passive current provider context form a
        pure provisional snapshot (ruling 29). Render registers nothing, starts nothing, writes
        nothing and emits no evidence. Its pre-attach send buffer holds 64 envelopes; the 65th
        throws AdmissionOverloaded unchanged. Attachment validates current providers and silently
        refreshes the baseline, then drains buffered events once in FIFO order into that same
        handle's actor, respecting ordinary capacity. An abandoned handle stays inert and loses
        its buffer without execution. This private bridge adds no public prepared lifecycle.
        Lookup useActorByRef(ref) returns Actor | null and never creates/adopts; an options object,
        including {}, means ensure (@plan.React.O2). SSR overlays are @plan.ServerRendering.O4.

        An action send(ref, publicEventOrFrom) declares a static edge to a shared actor. The
        payload mapper reads the sender's pre-turn snapshot/event; it cannot select a target or
        event type. Event/target provenance and payload validity are part of whole-batch staging:
        an invalid declaration rejects the sender's entire batch before commit. No target read,
        RPC, implicit ensure, lazy adoption or bidirectional context edge is granted.

        Target delivery is attempted after sender commit, without a pre-commit target-capacity
        reservation. Each valid send is then admitted to the target's FIFO as a later turn.
        Absent, disposing or disposed targets drop with SendTargetAbsent; overload rejects that
        delivery with AdmissionOverloaded. Both diagnostics belong to the sender and name sender
        incarnation, target and event; absent diagnostics also say whether the entry was adopted.
        They do not roll back the committed sender, cancel its other actions or mutate the target.
        Accepted delivery is bound to the then-current target incarnation; disposal before its
        decision retires it, never retargets it to a replacement. Several sends preserve their
        authored order; no cross-actor atomic transaction is implied.

        Every publication exposes one complete actor/store cut in the Runtime's single versioned
        store, including lane-, context-, lifecycle- and issue-only changes. Observers never see
        new store truth with the actor's prior snapshot. Committed evidence enters one global
        Runtime order after the snapshot; commit through publication/evidence acceptance is
        interruption-safe. Sink processing and user Effect settlement cannot hold publication.
        Readers keep their captured cut. A new snapshots subscription replays current truth
        without a read/subscribe gap; slow subscribers may skip intermediate snapshots. It emits
        terminal disposed truth and completes only at disposal, never at a terminal-looking leaf.

        Flow-owned records, arrays, envelopes and snapshots are copied/frozen; opaque application
        values keep identity. Public snapshots contain no revision, generation, raw Cause, input,
        timer inventory or acknowledgment; @plan.Machines.O1 and @plan.Inspection.O1 own their
        distinct surfaces. Time is integer epoch milliseconds from the Runtime Clock. Private
        revisions, generations, ordinals and evidence sequences are nonnegative safe integers,
        never reused; reserve terminal headroom before admission/restore, reject exhaustion before
        mutation, and never spend terminal reserves on ordinary work.
        Reference: references/dispatch-desk/app/host.ts,
        features/Tickets/editor/editor.machine.ts (Saved sends Toasts.Push).
    O5  Context propagation
        Provider.select(selector) is inert and preserves its exact selected result type. It reads
        one complete provider snapshot, including state, memory and lanes. A context slot binds
        once to one compatible shared ref and admitted incarnation in the same Runtime for the
        consumer's lifetime. Missing, ambiguous, foreign, mismatched, closing, disposed or cyclic
        providers reject atomically before effects. No forged local carrier or testing bypass
        satisfies production wiring. A context edge grants no parentage, ownership or send channel.

        Reads form an acyclic upward graph; cycles reject before admission. Establish the full
        provider-derived baseline silently at fresh admission, restore, lazy adoption and hydration;
        the first onContext selection emits nothing. A bound consumer counts as a provider use.
        Provider close prevents new bindings, and explicit provider disposal with bound consumers
        rejects before closing, naming every dependent and slot (@O6).

        Provider publications invalidate consumers in bounded dependency order. A context wakeup
        selects the latest complete provider snapshots when it executes, not an earlier queued
        selected value. Record exact provider incarnations and observed revisions in inspection.
        At most one combined context turn per dirty consumer occurs per settled wave. Completeness
        is causal closure for that consumer, including shared ancestors, not simultaneous
        publication across the entire graph. A diamond never combines a newly settled branch with
        unresolved old lineage from the same cause. Equal.equals governs slots and reaction
        selections; an equal branch settlement clears causal debt without a value-change event.

        A blocked context wakeup keeps its FIFO head position; ordinary later events cannot
        overtake it. Terminal disposal may retire the wakeup and queued work without upstream
        recovery. A changed complete context publishes once and reconciles continuing plans once;
        propagation never directly invokes a domain transition, memory patch or finite action.
        onContext declarations select context plus memory and enqueue ordinary events in declaration
        order under @plan.Machines.O7. Lane-only publication does not run the publishing actor's
        reactions, but consumers see its provider publication through this ordinary context path.

        A throwing consumer selector does not roll back the provider or siblings. That consumer
        keeps its last valid complete context, publishes one issue-only snapshot naming slot and
        provider revision, emits no context event and retries only on a newer provider revision,
        never the same bad revision. Capture observes completed waves: unresolved selected causal
        debt rejects with retryable ConcurrentCapture. Capture never advances time, creates work,
        forces context progress or silently waits for a wave to heal.
        Reference: references/dispatch-desk/features/Tickets/queue/queue.definition.ts,
        features/Tickets/queue/queue.machine.ts, features/Tickets/editor/editor.definition.ts.
    O6  Lifecycle and disposal
        Public lifecycle is exactly active, disposing or disposed. Pre-attach and bootstrap phases
        are private; suspension/resume is deleted. runtime.disposeActor(actor) validates Runtime
        ownership and disposes that actor for every user. Hooks observe terminal truth; mounted
        ensuring hooks may ensure a new incarnation after successful cleanup, while lookup-only
        hooks do not recreate one. Explicit provider disposal while consumers remain bound rejects
        unchanged with FlowError naming all dependents and slots; retain cannot expire a used
        provider. Once provider closing starts no new consumer edge can attach.

        Actor and Runtime disposal are asynchronous and idempotent. Repeated calls join the same
        cached result, never rerun cleanup. Runtime disposal closes all admission first, subsumes
        every actor and tears down consumers before providers. An actor closes its admission at
        one linearization point. Already committed publication finishes; undecided queued events,
        including a blocked context head, retire unexecuted. They change no domain truth, start no
        work and produce no TurnRecord. Private waiters settle once with terminal failure, never
        success. Local grace expiry is React's attachment policy (@plan.React.O3).

        Release activities, cancel schedules, stop subscriptions and queued unstarted work, and
        wait for invoked commits to settle without claiming an irreversible remote effect was
        undone. Normal actor disposal permits commits to settle. Runtime shutdown gives settlement
        and cleanup a total private 5,000 ms Clock budget, then interrupts remaining waits. An
        invoked commit without a known settlement becomes unknown with its original Attempt;
        fence all late callbacks. No hanging commit can keep runtime.dispose() pending forever.
        A failed or timed-out finalizer is cleanup failure, not successful release; report
        FlowError, quarantine the identity and keep the complete Cause. A successful dispose
        requires completed finalizers, accepted ordered terminal evidence, provider release and
        the final acknowledged persistence write. Failure to finish within the shutdown bound
        rejects rather than reporting a completed flush. No new public timeout option is added.

        Cleanup failure quarantines the shared ref for the Runtime: ensure and repeated dispose
        report the same cached failure without rerunning cleanup or creating another incarnation
        over leaked work. Post-visibility admission failures follow this rule too. Keep full Cause
        until cleanup ends; defect outranks failure, which outranks interruption, without losing
        the other causes (@plan.FlowDiagnostics.O3). Cause never enters serialized snapshots or traces.

        Publish complete disposing/disposed snapshots before their ordered lifecycle evidence,
        with causes distinguishing fresh admission, restore, attachment, owner disposal, retain
        expiry, React grace expiry and Runtime disposal. Disposal fabricates no machine event or
        terminal turn. The terminal snapshot retains the last committed state, memory and valid
        context. Internal invariant defects close actor admission, settle pending private waiters,
        finalize owned work and publish fatal disposed/cleanup truth with an issue, never a
        fabricated successful decision. Issue ownership is incarnation/source/binding; another
        lifetime cannot inherit or clear it.

        runtime.dispose({ forget: true }) uses @plan.FlowPersistence.O5's scoped deletion after closing
        admission; ordinary disposal preserves the last good durable scope. Locks release only
        after closeout. No leader election, mirroring, cross-tab sends or BroadcastChannel exists
        in version 1 (ruling 38); design/multi-tab.md remains deferred rationale only.
        Reference: references/dispatch-desk/app/host.ts, app/persistence.ts.
    O7  Isolation
        One Runtime owns its actors, adopted lazy set, dormant bytes, services, scopes, store,
        scheduler, clock, queues and evidence. Never share their mutable state with another
        Runtime, even for the same App, ref id or service tag. Pure compiled machine structure may
        be cached by exact machine object; compilation grants no shared execution authority.
        Runtime-owned refs/handles cannot resolve across Runtimes; nested React providers use the
        nearest Runtime, and foreign handles reject. Refs declared as inert module data can name
        independent actors in distinct Runtimes; this is not cross-Runtime lookup or ownership.

        A principal switch requires a new Runtime and persistence scope; use a keyed FlowProvider
        so consumers remount. Model authentication expiry as Session state, but never reinterpret
        old principal-owned actors, services or cache as a new user's data. Token-holder services
        updated through operations are ordinary Layers, not a global identity registry.

        Each server request and Story run constructs its own Runtime and service state. Never
        acquire a module-global request Runtime or request store. dehydrate owns a private
        nonpersistent request Runtime and disposes it before returning (@plan.ServerRendering.O1);
        services can be called directly for server redirect/notFound decisions. Hydration scope
        and App mismatches reject under @plan.ServerRendering.O6. Story storage is fresh memory
        storage, never browser or production storage (@plan.Testing.O7).
        Reference: none yet (stage S10 adds it).
decisions
    D1  Construction returns values and throws FlowError
        Was FlowState D8 and D10, applied to app, setup, refs, admission and host boundaries.
        Canonical diagnostics preserve paths and classification; deleted error-class names do
        not return. @plan.FlowDiagnostics.O1 and @plan.FlowDiagnostics.O2 own shape and closed codes.
    D2  Authored identity is exact
        Was FlowState D9 (Runtime identity part). Keep UTF-8 limits and the string actor wire
        form; ruling 35 adds unambiguously encoded readonly id parts. App keys are not durable ids.
    D3  Shared by ref, local by owner
        Was FlowState D12, from minimal-library section 1. ensure/create/dispose replace leases,
        host getActor and story.actor. Incarnations, ref conflicts and disposing waits follow
        rulings 8 and 9; provider uses and quarantine are binding.
    D4  The app lists machines
        Was FlowState D13. module and tooling ownership identities are removed. Declared lazy
        entries preserve a closed universe under ruling 31, not arbitrary registration.
    D5  Wiring lives on refs or app defaults
        Was FlowState D14, from minimal-library section 2. Local actors inherit defaults; shared
        refs override declared slots. Each live binding fixes one provider incarnation.
    D6  Services are Layers
        Was FlowState D15. No Implementation, ImplementationError or succeed/effect/merge
        compatibility values. Ruling 7 preserves production Layer merge semantics; storage
        acquisition precedes detached restore validation, then remaining services (@O2).
    D7  Lifecycle has three public states
        Was FlowState D16. active, disposing, disposed; no public prepared or suspended state.
        Ruling 29 restores pure provisional handles and a 64-event buffer, not runtime preparation
        during render. Bounded Runtime cleanup follows ruling 22, with a 5,000 ms private budget.
    D8  Actor messaging is asynchronous and visible
        Was FlowState D27, from minimal-library section 9. Shared targets and public events only;
        target admission occurs after sender commit (@O4), with sender-owned failure diagnostics.
    D9  Selector defects isolate consumers
        Was FlowState D28, closing ApplicationsAndActors Q1. Provider and sibling truth remains
        committed; only the defective consumer retains old context and retries on a newer revision.
    D10  An options object means ensure
        Was FlowState D51. useActorByRef(ref, {}) ensures an input-free actor; the no-options form
        only looks up. runtime.ensureActor(ref) remains valid for input-free machines. Ruling 29
        requires a handle from every ensuring hook even before attach; no hook suspends.
    D11  Second-tab detection replaces shared multi-tab persistence
        Ruling 38 supersedes rulings 32 and 33's multi-tab adoption: ifAvailable lock at ready,
        held until disposal; refusal means no restore/capture and SecondTabNoPersistence. No locks
        support means no check. There is no MultiTab plan dependency in version 1.
constraints
    C1  One production execution path
        Hosts, requests, React, Stories and CLI use the same Runtime, store, admission, ordering,
        context and finalization. Fixtures supply services, not replacement kernels or evidence.
    C2  No substitute public machinery
        No leases, host lookup, acknowledged dispatch, runtime registration, child actors,
        generic registries, machine.provide, public ManagedRuntime, boot/decoder API or second
        scheduler/store. Hydrate/dehydrate are only the dedicated server-rendering boundaries.
    C3  Private composition stays replaceable
        Cell/queue representation and service topology are private; inert construction, exact type
        closure, bounded admission, causal context, atomic cuts, incarnation fencing, no replay,
        honest uncertainty and reverse cleanup are binding. Use the pinned toolchain; never edit
        the frozen package or erase public type information to satisfy a proof.
verification
    V1  Closed graph and identity
        Planned construction/type fixtures: exact app key inference; duplicate key/id/value;
        unknown fields; lazy id mismatch, duplicate module object and undeclared machine; app-key
        rename keeps durable identity. ASCII and 2/3/4-byte ref vectors prove byte lengths, exact
        spelling and string/parts domain separation; ["a/b", "c"] never equals ["a", "b/c"].
        All constructors run no loader, service, storage, actor, timer or evidence.
    V2  Service closure
        Planned isolated and packed consumers infer adapter requirements through eager and lazy
        machines; omit services only for empty requirements, exclude only Flow-owned Scope,
        accept supersets, reject missing/remaining inputs and recursive expansion. Record compile
        time for small and medium apps without assertions or excessive-instantiation errors.
        Prove once-per-Runtime storage/application acquisition, merge precedence and scoped release.
    V3  Readiness races and bootstrap
        Planned production gates control storage acquisition, detached validation, app acquisition,
        provider-first install, baselines and activation. Test acquisition failure versus corrupt
        scope recovery, reverse rollback, cached failure, two ready waiters with one interrupted,
        dispose racing late acquisition, and no partial handles/evidence. Same-scope readiness in
        two browsing contexts grants one lock and makes the other nonpersistent; no-lock hosts
        proceed. SSR render remains inert and obeys Hydrate's first-render rule.
    V4  Admission, retention and reincarnation
        Planned production tests: shared ensure joins, first input wins with differing-input
        diagnostic, conflicting ref metadata rejects unchanged, local create is always distinct,
        clean invisible failure permits new input, provider race rejects before registration.
        Ensure during disposal waits then creates once; failed cleanup quarantines. Advance TestClock
        across retain, reacquire/reset, provider use, restored unused retain and expiry. Deliver old
        outcome, timer, issue clear and send-back after re-ensure: none touches the new incarnation.
    V5  Mailboxes, sends and atomic reads
        Planned tests saturate 1,024 pending facts and 64 pre-attach sends: overflow changes
        nothing; invalid tag/payload/internal event admits no turn; attach drains once; abandoned
        render stays inert. Reentrant and multi-sender delivery is FIFO. Test valid send alongside
        other sender actions with absent, overloaded, disposing and replaced targets; failures
        belong to sender after its commit, with no rollback or implicit adoption. Exercise internal
        producer pressure, bounded source failure and reserved terminal capacity. Several readers
        see one actor/store version; slow subscribers skip safely; new subscriptions replay without
        a gap; terminal stream emits once then completes. Exhaust counters before mutation while
        preserving terminal headroom. A send cycle is warned statically and bounded only in Stories.
    V6  Causal context
        Planned chain and diamond tests prove per-consumer closure, equal settlement clearing debt,
        one wakeup per wave, latest complete values, Equal.equals and FIFO blocking. One selector
        defect preserves provider/siblings and old consumer context, emits one issue, retries only
        on a newer revision. Bindings stay fixed; completed-wave capture succeeds, debt rejects
        ConcurrentCapture without running work. onContext emission order agrees with Machines O7.
    V7  Terminal ownership and isolation
        Planned gates: provider-with-consumers rejection names every slot; new binding races close;
        repeated dispose shares one result; blocked context/queued events retire without turns;
        known commits settle before close, hanging commits become unknown within 5,000 ms; failed
        or timed-out finalizer quarantines with full Cause and no rerun. Successful disposal waits
        for evidence and acknowledged last write; timeout cannot claim flush success. Snapshots
        keep committed truth, and lifecycle causes distinguish owner/retain/grace/Runtime disposal.
        Two Runtimes, principals, requests and Stories share no mutable truth, services or storage;
        foreign handles reject. Run scoped runtime tests, required type gates, packed consumers
        and stage checks before claiming these proposed guarantees implemented.
questions
    Q1  What execution form preserves ensure's wait while an incarnation is disposing?
        Ruling 9 requires waiting then creating; ordinary ensureActor is synchronous and
        create/ensure normally expose active handles. A synchronous call cannot await an Effect
        finalizer. Recommended: a stable private pending handle using the existing pure pre-attach
        snapshot and bounded buffer, attached after cleanup; no new public lifecycle or union return.
        The type/runtime spike must settle this exceptional form before S3 implementation. Until
        then the waiting and fencing guarantees in @O3 bind; callers must never resurrect the old
        incarnation or silently fail the wait as NotReady.
    Q2  Can an opaque services Layer support storage-first selective acquisition?
        The required order in @O2 is explicit, but a single opaque merged Layer may not expose
        independently buildable storage and application roots. Recommended: prove storage-output
        projection plus shared memoization against Effect 4.0.5 in S1; if it builds unrelated
        services early, obtain an owner ruling on whole-Layer-first acquisition or a split storage
        dependency boundary. Do not implement validation before acquiring its KeyValueStore.
